 comfortable.\"\n2. (adjective) \"Cooked and intended to be eaten while still warm.\"\n3. (adjective) \"Having a strong taste that causes a burning feeling in the mouth.\"\n4. (adjective) \"Very popular, fashionable, or in great demand.\"\n5. (adjective) \"Causing strong disagreement, excitement, or public interest.\"\n6. (adjective) \"Sexually attractive.\"\n7. (adjective) \"Involving intense activity, competition, or emotion.\"\n8. (adjective) \"Newly obtained or produced and very recent.\"\n9. (adjective) \"Very bright and strong in colour.\"\n10. (adjective) \"Very skilful at a particular activity or knowledgeable about a particular subject.\"\n11. (adjective) \"Stolen and therefore risky to possess or sell.\"\n12. (adjective) \"Dangerous or likely to cause trouble.\"\n13. (adjective) \"Angry or easily made angry.\"\n14. (adjective) \"Very likely to win or succeed.\"\n15. (adjective) \"Very interested in or eager for something.\"\n16. (adjective) \"Feeling strong sexual desire.\"\n17. (adjective) \"Containing sexual material that is intended to excite people.\"\n18. (adjective) \"Strict or insistent about making sure a rule or standard is followed.\"\n19. (adjective) \"Recent enough to provide promising clues about someone or something being sought.\"\n20. (adjective) \"Very close to finding a hidden object or guessing the correct answer in a game.\"\n21. (adjective) \"Carrying an electric current that could cause an electric shock.\"\nExtra senses: none.\nEach sense was reviewed on its own; judge the list, not the wording of a gloss.\nJudge only the word spelled exactly \"hot\". A sense that belongs to a spelling differing in capitalization, or to a proper name spelled otherwise, belongs to that other entry and is never missing from this one.\npass only when every one of these holds:\n- Complete: common has every sense a learner''s dictionary lists among its main senses of this word for A1 to B1 learners, in every part of speech it has. A missing main sense fails; a sense only an advanced or specialist dictionary lists does not.\n- Distinct: no two common senses are duplicates, or one meaning split too finely into several.\n- Ordered: common runs in a plausible order from more to less common use for a learner, judged sense by sense across every part of speech, never one part of speech before another. The first common sense is the one a learner most needs: \"mobile\" starts with the noun meaning a mobile phone, before any adjective sense. Frequency order is approximate: judge only whether the order is plausible, never whether exact frequency can be verified.\n- Rare: specialist, technical, legal, archaic, and rare senses, and senses used only in a variety other than British or American English, belong in extra (\"stay\" meaning to stop legal proceedings, \"mobile\" meaning changing facial expression, Scottish \"stay\" meaning to live somewhere), and every sense in extra is one a learner does not meet in general use.\nA rare, archaic, Old English, technical, or specialist sense missing from both lists is never a failure.\nfail when any of them does not hold. The reason then names everything to change, even if that takes more than one sentence: each missing common sense with its part of speech and a short meaning, each pair of duplicates by number, each sense out of order, each specialist sense in common, and each sense in extra that is common.\nuncertain: only when you cannot judge the word at all. Never answer uncertain because the exact frequency order cannot be verified: a plausible order passes."}},"context":{"subject":{"lemma":"hot","senses":[{"id":"98cdca81-7f89-42aa-85c5-fd6d3f57fe24","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Having a high temperature or a temperature higher than is comfortable."},{"id":"16750b2c-f8a8-4507-b4a4-03783943275a","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Recent enough to provide promising clues about someone or something being sought."},{"id":"2cb3f0a5-6141-4003-a231-aab994fb1cd3","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Very close to finding a hidden object or guessing the correct answer in a game."},{"id":"94c15bba-939d-4361-b023-b2670fa6d093","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Having a strong taste that causes a burning feeling in the mouth."},{"id":"e85f4f2d-a8ab-436e-8a78-ced0b44b5f0b","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Very popular, fashionable, or in great demand."},{"id":"b108ed5f-d185-4405-ba3f-7b1974e7055a","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Causing strong disagreement, excitement, or public interest."},{"id":"0973bf32-2e58-4d79-bf30-2bc93ea64ab1","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Sexually attractive."},{"id":"d55090eb-2440-4a6b-b6f2-5f9ae8a836db","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Feeling strong sexual desire."},{"id":"7903d479-a65b-41bc-88d5-b37ad028e249","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Stolen and therefore risky to possess or sell."},{"id":"da21b5a3-7493-4750-bf44-c0d930b1a9bb","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Very skilful at a particular activity or knowledgeable about a particular subject."},{"id":"2e6b2bf5-d455-46f4-b83b-f56ac1223b9a","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Newly obtained or produced and very recent."},{"id":"e332ae64-98d1-4cd8-a161-38cad95ef2d7","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Involving intense activity, competition, or emotion."},{"id":"2ddd5d75-ae1b-49d3-8339-61081edfb6ae","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Very interested in or eager for something."},{"id":"5944d518-4b8c-45a9-a033-67146bdd5df4","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Very likely to win or succeed."},{"id":"7f266b27-987e-4713-b56d-cb60bad7cee2","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Strict or insistent about making sure a rule or standard is followed."},{"id":"48aa8766-14c4-410c-8a55-0926f694bf20","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Carrying an electric current that could cause an electric shock."},{"id":"83277e0f-619e-4587-91ed-9b44578cb919","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Dangerous or likely to cause trouble."},{"id":"b0bd6bd7-4d59-4998-80a2-13434a000a96","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Very bright and strong in colour."},{"id":"27a90d68-edf1-48c5-ac74-8ce1c2217a78","pos":"adjective","gloss":"Containing se